  <h2>Context</h2>
  <p>
    ZHIVA is a working jewellery business on Shopify. I handle organic search:
    the architecture of collections and products, the technical layer underneath
    them, and the content that has to rank.
  </p>

  <h2>The problem</h2>
  <p>
    Shopify is a good platform to sell on and an opinionated one to do search work
    on. Several structural decisions are baked in, produce duplication by default,
    and cannot be configured away.
  </p>
  <p>
    A product is reachable at both <code>/products/handle</code> and
    <code>/collections/x/products/handle</code>. Filter and sort parameters each
    generate a crawlable URL, so colour, material, price band and sort order
    multiply into combinations that all serve subsets of the same products.
    Paginated collection pages repeat the title, description and intro copy of page
    one. None of it is a bug; it is what generality looks like from the inside.
  </p>

  <h2>The approach</h2>
  <p>
    Three decisions did most of the work.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Internal links point at the canonical form.</strong> Collection cards
    link to <code>/products/</code> directly rather than the long path. Shopify
    already canonicalises, but the default templates meant the site was largely
    linking to URLs that get canonicalised away — one unnecessary hop on every
    internal path, and analytics split across several page paths per product.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Filtered URLs have to earn indexation.</strong> Non-indexable by
    default, indexable by exception — and only where a filter matches genuine
    search demand. A material filter can deserve a real landing page; a sort order
    never does.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Collections map to demand, not to merchandising convenience.</strong>
    Someone searching a category is looking for a collection page, not a product.
    A collection that exists only because it was convenient to group those items in
    admin is an internal tool, not a landing page — and two collections sharing
    overlapping products with nothing on them but the grid are near-identical
    documents competing for related queries.
  </p>

  <h2>My role</h2>
  <p>
    Search architecture and organic growth across products and collections,
    including the content that sits on collection pages and the technical
    decisions about what gets crawled and indexed.
  </p>

  <h2>Status</h2>
  <p>Live and ongoing.</p>
  <p>
    The general version of this is written up in
    <a href="/writing/shopify-seo-architecture">Shopify SEO: the duplication
    problems that are built into the platform</a>.
  </p>
</Work>
